  "summary": "A single-helix steel rotor turns inside a double-helix rubber stator. Because the stator pitch is twice the rotor pitch, a chain of sealed cavities forms along the pump; as the rotor turns (rolling eccentrically, its axis orbiting the stator axis), the cavities progress from suction to discharge without changing shape or volume. The result is smooth, low-shear, nearly pulse-free flow proportional to speed — ideal for sludges, pastes, viscous and shear-sensitive products, and liquids with solids or gas.",
  "classification": {
    "family": "Positive displacement – rotary",
    "configuration": "Progressive cavity (Moineau), 1:2 rotor/stator lobe geometry, 2 stages"
  },
  "model_configuration": {
    "eccentricity_mm": 11.0,
    "rotor_pitch_mm": 160.0,
    "stator_pitch_mm": 320.0,
    "stages": 2,
    "pressure_note": "Pressure capability per stage depends on the design (often of the order of 6 bar per stage — verify with manufacturer)."
  },
